      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Illustrates the step-by-step process of solving the linear equation 10q=44 and verifying its solution.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{10q}{10} = \frac{44}{10}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide both sides by 10 to undo the multiplication.</cell>
              <cell>$$q = \frac{22}{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$q = \frac{22}{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check:</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Substitute $$10(\frac{22}{5})\overset{?}{=}44$$ into the original equation.</cell>
              <cell>$$\overset{2}{10}\left( \frac{22}{5} \right)\overset{?}{=}44$$</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$44 = 44\ ✓$$</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{22}{5}.$$</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The solution to the equation was the fraction $$12u = -76.$$ We leave it as an improper fraction.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
