    <subsection title="Solve Equations with a Fraction Coefficient">
      <p>When we have an equation with a fraction coefficient we can use the Multiplication Property of Equality to make the coefficient equal to $$\frac{3}{4}x = 24$$</p>
      <p>For example, in the equation:</p>
      <equation>$$x$$</equation>
      <p>The coefficient of $$\frac{3}{4}.$$ is $$x,$$ To solve for $$1.$$ we need its coefficient to be $$1,$$ Since the product of a number and its reciprocal is $$x$$ our strategy here will be to isolate $$\frac{3}{4}.$$ by multiplying by the reciprocal of $$\frac{3}{4}x = 24.$$ We will do this in Example 4.102.</p>
      <example number="4.102">
        <problem>
          <p>Solve: $$x = 32$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply both sides by the reciprocal of the coefficient.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Check:</cell>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Substitute $$32$$.</cell>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Rewrite $$\frac{3}{4}x = 24,$$ as a fraction.</cell>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply. The equation is true.</cell>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>Notice that in the equation $$\frac{3}{4}$$ we could have divided both sides by $$x$$ to get $$\frac{2}{5}n = 14.$$ by itself. Dividing is the same as multiplying by the reciprocal, so we would get the same result. But most people agree that multiplying by the reciprocal is easier.</p>
        </solution>
      </example>
